# shellcheck shell=bash
# Invocation behind output/evaluation_vad_refine/ (the timestamp benchmark
# with its benchmark-only --vad-refine option, which extends Whisper's
# segment ends with Silero VAD; the manuscript does not report it).
#
# Job 8917981, 2026-09-27, at code 6f9bf56 with a clean tree, on an NVIDIA
# H100 80GB HBM3 (node htc-g059, account <project-b>), with Silero VAD
# loaded from the tag snakers4/silero-vad:v6.2.1 (vad_source in the
# summary). Its environment record is in environment/au_ts_vad_8917981.txt
# beside the original's results on ARC.
#
# The job reproduced the first committed run, job 7746221 (2026-05-31, a
# Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB on htc-g033, code bb32915, Silero from the untagged
# repository), in every row and every column that run wrote. The command
# below names the card for the reason timestamps.env gives.
#
# MODEL and LIMIT below are the script's defaults at 6f9bf56, written out
# so that a later change of a default cannot change what this record
# reproduces. VAD_REFINE and REPORT_DIR are not: the defaults are 0 and
# the evaluation directory itself. torch.hub downloads the tag on first
# use, so a node without network access needs the hub cache warmed with it
# beforehand.
#
# Reproduce (from the repository root on ARC):
#   ( set -a; . hpc/runs/timestamps_vad.env || exit 1; set +a
#     sbatch --account='<your-project>' --gres=gpu:h100:1 --constraint=nvlink \
#       hpc/submit_benchmark_timestamps.sh )
#
# A reproduction writes to its own directory under repro/, so it cannot
# overwrite the ARC copy of the original run it is to be compared with.
# original: REPORT_DIR="${DATA}/speech_transcription_env/output/evaluation/audit2026/timestamps_vad"
MODEL=openai/whisper-large-v3
LIMIT=500
VAD_REFINE=1
REPORT_DIR="${DATA}/speech_transcription_env/output/evaluation/repro/timestamps_vad"
